|> Exercise
    !! work=ing
    handle=_cosine_rule_via_jacks_rule_

    Keeping a triangle with sides $a$, $b$, $c$
    !! opposite to which are angles 
    with opposing angles
    $\theta_{bc}$, $\theta_{ac}$, $\theta_{ab}$
    respectively, prove _Jack's rule_, which is the triplet of equations...

    $$
    \begin{gathered}
    a\cos\theta_{ac} + b\cos\theta_{bc} = c \\
    b\cos\theta_{ab} + c\cos\theta_{ac} = a \up{1.4}\\
    a\cos\theta_{ab} + c\cos\theta_{bc} = b \up{1.4}
    \end{gathered}
    $$

    ...and then prove the _cosine rule_, which is the equation...

    $$
    c^2 = a^2 + b^2  - 2ab\cos\theta_{ab}
    $$

    ...by manipulating the equations in$\rt{0.1}$ Jack's rule.

    |> Solution

        Because the equations in$\rt{0.1}$ Jack's rule are
        symmetric it suffices to prove the first equation.
        We need to consider two cases according to
        whether 
        side $c$ is adjacent to $0$ or $1$
        obtuse angles 
        (the two sub-cases in which there is $1$ obtuse
        angle are symmetric to one another, we consider only one):

        |> Image
            src=images/_e5_cosine_rule_via_jacks_two_cases.svg

        In Case 1 we obviously have

        $$
        c = a\cos\theta_{ac} + b\cos\theta_{bc}
        $$

        verifying$\rt{0.1}$ Jack's rule,
        whereas, in Case 2, note that

        $$
        -a\cos\theta_{ab}
        $$

        is positive—it's a _length_ as the diagram
        indicates—so

        $$
        c = b\cos\theta_{bc} - (-a\cos\theta_{ac}) = b\cos\theta_{bc} + a\cos\theta_{ac}
        $$

        again verifying$\rt{0.1}$ Jack's rule, 
        and which completes the proof thereof. 
        |> InlineImage
            src=images/_e5_cosine_rule_via_jacks_JACK_logo_.svg
            style=transform:translate(0,-3px)

        Next, for the algebraic manipulation of the equations,
        start by multiplying each equation in$\rt{0.1}$ Jack's rule
        by its own right-hand side, obtaining...

        $$
        \begin{gathered}
        ac\cos\theta_{ac} + bc\cos\theta_{bc} = c^2 \\
        ab\cos\theta_{ab} + ac\cos\theta_{ac} = a^2 \up{1.4}\\
        ab\cos\theta_{ab} + bc\cos\theta_{bc} = b^2 \up{1.4}
        \end{gathered}
        $$
        |> ImageRight
            src=images/_e5_cosine_rule_via_jacks_multiplying_cloud_.svg
            offset_x=0.5em

        !! $$
        !! \begin{gathered}
        !! (a\cos\theta_{ac} + b\cos\theta_{bc} = c) * c \\
        !! (b\cos\theta_{ab} + c\cos\theta_{ac} = a) * a \up{1.4}\\
        !! (a\cos\theta_{ab} + c\cos\theta_{bc} = b) * b \up{1.4}
        !! \end{gathered}
        !! $$

        ...but then if we subtract the last two equations from
        the first we obtain...
        |> ImageLeft
            src=images/_e5_cosine_rule_via_jacks_equation_adding_cloud.svg
            offset_x=0.5em

        $$
        -2ab\cos\theta_{ab} = c^2 - a^2 - b^2
        $$


        ...which is the cosine rule, rearranged!
